# Line endings: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every machine.
#
# Why this file exists, rather than trusting everyone's local git config:
#
# On 2026-09-08, in a sibling repo in this project, nine files came back from a
# Windows machine carrying CRLF. An 84-line change arrived as 3,716 lines of
# diff -- a day's real work all but buried in line-ending churn, and every one of
# those "changed" lines byte-identical apart from an invisible \r. Reviewing that
# honestly is not possible; reviewing it dishonestly is what actually happens.
#
# This repo is LF-clean today, but nothing enforced it -- it was luck plus a
# hardened local config on two machines. It now has contributors on two machines
# and possibly a third, and a local config is exactly the thing a new clone,
# a new machine, or a new person does not have. So the rule lives in the repo,
# where it travels with the code and applies to whoever shows up.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate and does more than `text=auto` alone would. text=auto
# stops CRLF getting *committed*; eol=lf also stops Windows *checking out* CRLF
# against LF blobs, which is the other half of the same failure -- it produces a
# whole-tree phantom diff of files that were never touched.

# Default: git detects text vs binary by content, normalises text to LF in the
# repository, and checks it out as LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Text, stated explicitly so intent survives a future rename or addition ---
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# SVG is XML. It looks like an asset and lives beside the images, but it is text
# and should be normalised and diffable like any other source file.
*.svg   text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never normalise, never diff as text ---
# Touching line endings in these corrupts them. The fonts and the logo raster
# are the ones actually in this repo; the rest are listed so that dropping one
# in later does not silently become a normalisation bug.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
